### Summary

In a medium-sized private React app, upgrading to `better-auth@1.6.9` as part of an auth stack upgrade caused TypeScript-based checks to run out of memory while inferring the client returned by `createAuthClient(...)` from `better-auth/react`.

The runtime behavior appears fine. The issue is the type graph created by the inferred client type when plugins are passed to `createAuthClient`.

### Environment

- Node: 24.x
- TypeScript: 6.0.3
- better-auth: 1.6.9
- React: 19.2.x
- @tanstack/react-query: 5.101.x
- kitcn: 0.15.10
- Vite: 8.0.x

The relevant client setup shape is:

```ts
import { createAuthClient } from "better-auth/react";
import { adminClient } from "better-auth/client/plugins";
import { convexClient } from "kitcn/auth/client";
import { crossDomainClient } from "@convex-dev/better-auth/client/plugins";

export const authClient = createAuthClient({
  baseURL: siteUrl,
  plugins: [crossDomainClient(), convexClient(), adminClient()],
});
```

The app then imports `authClient` from React components and uses a small subset of the client surface, mostly:

```ts
authClient.useSession();
authClient.signIn.email(...);
authClient.signOut();
authClient.getCookie?.();
```

### Evidence

I bisected this by changing only the auth stack on top of an otherwise working dependency tree.

With the previous auth stack:

- `better-auth@1.5.3`
- `kitcn@0.12.26`
- same app code

`tsc -b && vite build` completed successfully:

- max RSS: about 2.3 GB
- wall time: about 2 minutes

After upgrading only the auth stack to:

- `better-auth@1.6.9`
- `kitcn@0.15.10`

`tsc -b && vite build` failed with Node heap OOM:

```text
FATAL ERROR: Ineffective mark-compacts near heap limit Allocation failed - JavaScript heap out of memory
Aborted (core dumped)
```

Measured peak before abort:

- max RSS: about 4.37 GB
- wall time: about 4 minutes
- exit status: 134

A type-aware ESLint run over the same frontend also OOMed before it could report lint diagnostics.

### Workaround that fixed the OOM

Putting an explicit runtime type boundary around `createAuthClient` fixed the issue without changing runtime behavior:

```ts
type FrontendAuthSession = {
  user: {
    email: string;
    name: string;
    userId?: string;
    role?: string | null;
  };
};

type FrontendAuthClient = {
  getCookie?: () => string;
  signIn: {
    email: (variables: { email: string; password: string }) => Promise<unknown>;
  };
  signOut: () => Promise<unknown>;
  useSession: () => {
    data: FrontendAuthSession | null;
    isPending: boolean;
  };
};

type RuntimeAuthFactory = (options: { baseURL: string; plugins: unknown[] }) => unknown;

const createRuntimeAuthClient = createAuthClient as unknown as RuntimeAuthFactory;
const fullAuthClient = createRuntimeAuthClient({
  baseURL: siteUrl,
  plugins: [crossDomainClient(), convexClient(), adminClient()],
});

export const authClient = fullAuthClient as FrontendAuthClient;
```

With that boundary in place, the same upgraded dependency tree completed successfully:

- `tsc -b && vite build`: max RSS about 2.2 GB
- frontend typecheck under an 8 GB memory cap: max RSS about 3.1 GB
- type-aware frontend ESLint: max RSS about 2.6 GB

### Expected behavior

A React app should be able to call `createAuthClient({ plugins: [...] })` and export the resulting client without TypeScript needing multiple GB of heap or aborting near the default Node heap limit.

It would be helpful if the public client type could be bounded or simplified enough that consumers do not need to erase the factory/plugin type graph manually when they only use a small part of the client surface.

### Notes

I realize the repro includes integration plugins from outside this repository. I am reporting here first because the memory spike disappears when the `better-auth/react` `createAuthClient` inference is hidden behind a runtime boundary, while the same runtime plugin stack still works.

