### To Reproduce

`export const auth = betterAuth({
	user: {
		additionalFields: {
			status: {
				type: "string",
				defaultValue: "PENDING",
				input: false,
			},
			finishedRegistration: {
				type: "boolean",
				defaultValue: false,
				input: false,
			},
		},
	},
} satisfies BetterAuthOptions);`

`import Elysia from "elysia";
import { auth, type Session } from "./auth";
import { UnauthorizedError } from "./errors";

export const authHandle = new Elysia({
	name: "auth-handle",
})
	.mount(auth.handler)
	.macro({
		isActiveUser: {
			async resolve({ request: { headers } }) {
				const session = (await auth.api.getSession({
					headers,
				})) as Session | null;

				if (!session) throw new UnauthorizedError("USER_NOT_ALLOWED");

				if (
					session?.user.status !== "ACTIVE" ||
					!session.user.finishedRegistration
				)
					throw new UnauthorizedError("USER_NOT_ACTIVATED");

				return {
					user: session.user,
					session: session.session,
				};
			},
		},
	});
`
The problem occurs because betterAuth() returns a runtime instance, but the $Infer type helpers (like auth.$Infer.Session) are not exposed as proper TypeScript types that can be referenced statically.

In TypeScript, typeof auth.$Infer.Session doesn’t actually resolve to the expected InferSession<typeof auth> type — instead, it collapses to any because $Infer exists only at runtime (as a helper or placeholder object).

This means that the type inference pipeline breaks — the compiler has no link between the runtime auth object and its generic type definitions.

So, even though the object at runtime has session.user and session.session correctly structured, TypeScript can’t infer their compile-time types.

